# Rust
target/
Cargo.lock

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.log

# OS / Editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

# Environment
*.env
.env
# Every .env VARIANT, not just the two spellings above. `*.env` only matches a
# name ENDING in ".env", so `.env.dev-remote` — which holds live DEV Postgres and
# Neo4j credentials — was tracked-eligible until this line. Suffixed variants are
# the normal shape here (.env.local, .env.dev-remote), so the pattern has to lead
# with the dot, not end with it (Standing Rule 2 / CLAUDE.md §22, §26).
.env.*
# The two committed templates are the deliberate exception: they carry key NAMES
# and no values, and new environments are bootstrapped from them.
!.env.example
!*.env.example

# Generated artifacts
uploads/
extracted_text/
exports/

# Python or temp
__pycache__/
*.pyc

docs/save/

# ONNX model cache (large binary files)
.fastembed_cache/
backend/.env.local

# Linux desktop entry files (e.g., from Neo4j Desktop install)
*.desktop

# Working artifacts for the codebase test audit (Roman decides later
# whether these stay in the repo or move elsewhere).
backend/test_audit/
# Ignore local .claude state (settings.local.json, backups, caches) but TRACK the
# enforcement agent definitions so the four-agent gate is identical on every
# machine/CI. A wholesale-ignored dir can't re-include a child, so the parent must
# be `.claude/*` (not `.claude/`) for the `!` negation to take effect.
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
.venv/
*_output.txt
query-*.json
run_*.py

# One-shot maintenance tools write their count proof beside the checkout
# (`practice_reset_report.txt`, `practice_deck_seed_report.txt`, …). The proof is
# real output an operator reads and keeps — it is just not source, and six of
# them have been sitting untracked in `backend/` since the merge tooling ran.
*_report.txt
